# Marta, who hates an unfinished story.
dialogue marta4
  node root marta "Clerk! Sunburnt and full of story. Sit down and tell it. Start with the pirates."
    > "I found my father." if not seen:marta4.father -> father
    > "The story isn't finished yet." if not seen:marta4.unfinished -> unfinished
    > "Would you preside over an inquiry?" if seen:bramwick4.paper and not seen:marta4.preside -> preside
    > "Goodnight, Marta." -> bye
  node father marta "You never. (She puts down the glass. She's been polishing it since the Marrow sailed.) Alive? Where is he?"
    -> where
  node where marta "On the Marrow? Under that yellow rag? Cray ran it up before his boat was tied. It hasn't moved all night. Neither have I."
    > "Then I'd better get the rag down." -> rag
  node rag marta "You'd better. And when it's done, I want it told here. Every word. I've been owed this one three years."
  node unfinished marta "Then finish it. I hate an unfinished story. Finish it here, where I can see. Don't skip to the end."
  node preside marta "In my tavern? Bring me the villain and the evidence. I'll bring the cat. Tallow left it here at supper. It runs the bar now."
    note marta_preside "Marta will preside over an inquiry at the Keel, if I bring her the villain and the evidence. She'll bring the cat."
  node bye marta "Go quickly. You've a bell after you. I won't have a story end in my doorway."
  # Once the inquiry's done.
  node after marta "Well. That's an ending. No, it isn't. Not until it's in the book. Give Bramwick back his stamp. I'll have the room watching."
  # Once the flag's down and Bramwick has lent the stamp back.
  node book marta "Flag's down. You've the stamp and the ledger. The bar's right there. I've the whole room watching."
  # Once the ledger's written.
  node ending marta "You've told it in ink. Now take it up the steps. Onn's owed it before I am. Then go and find your navigator."
    -> gull
  node gull marta "She's been on that pier since dawn. Waiting."
  node cards marta "Hush, clerk. He's bluffing. He's always bluffing. Thirty years I've let him think I can't tell."
